build/

fatest1.txt
FATEST2.TXT

# written by `make`: which image the native engine runs (Iteration 397)
.relf-native-img

# Also written by `make`: the host architecture the engines were built
# for, so a checkout moved to another machine rebuilds them (402). It
# was tracked, so a build on the ARMv7 board dirtied the tree (489).
.relf-arch

# The shell images are build products: `make`, or relfsh itself, makes
# them from kernel.img and the sources in seconds. Untracked at 489;
# tests/verify records a checksum of each instead, so a rebuild on
# another machine is still compared with this one's. The two KERNEL
# images stay tracked - cross.4 runs on kernel.img to produce it, so
# they are the bootstrap seed and cannot be rebuilt from nothing.
kernel-shell.img
kernel32-shell.img

# The engines are build products - 40 KB of C through cc. They were
# TRACKED until Iteration 402, which put an x86-64 binary in an ARMv7
# checkout, where `make` saw a file newer than cv8.c and used it:
#   ./relfsh: line 144: relf: cannot execute binary file: Exec format error
relf
relf32

# Python bytecode. tests/interactive/__pycache__ was TRACKED until
# Iteration 407: three .pyc files, regenerated on every run, that
# turned up as modifications in `git status` on any machine with a
# different Python (Iteration 407).
__pycache__/
*.py[co]

# What the suites leave behind: yash's harness writes a .trs beside each
# test file, the differential and matrix runners work in mktemp
# directories, and tests/verify writes its logs to /tmp - but a run
# interrupted mid-way can leave these here.
*.trs
*.tmp
boot.log
core

# Editor and patch leftovers
*~
*.orig
*.rej
.*.swp

# The shells are build products since Iteration 506: the engine with its
# shell image inside (tools/embed.sh). relfsh was a script until then.
relfsh
relfsh32
